# ENGINE-UNIVERSAL-SERVICES.17 slice 8 — S2: S1 with the RESCUING alternative removed, so the
# guard is the only thing that can produce the verdict.
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND IT IS A DEFECT IN S1's ROW SET THAT A PLANT FOUND. S1's entry rule
# has two alternatives:
#
#   scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi        # the residual-BEARING holder — needs the guard
#          | kw_k eq prim semi                # the residual-FREE holder — must stay unguarded
#
# and the second one can parse e1–e6 ON ITS OWN, through the eliminated-but-unguarded `prim`.
# So every S1 arm-B row except `e7` passes under BOTH hypotheses: with the guard and without it.
# Measured, not reasoned: deleting the trailing-lookahead emission in `apply_plan` and re-running the
# whole bank left it **GREEN, 35/35**. A plant that cannot fail is a row that cannot check.
#
# ⭐ `.17` slice 4 had already recorded exactly this, one ladder down — `guard_effectiveness/probe.sh`
# runs `g4` and `g5` on `e1` and `e7` ONLY, *"because their second alternative absorbs e2–e6 on its
# own, so those rows would pass under BOTH hypotheses and prove nothing"*. The two-holder shape is
# discriminating for CALL-SITE SCOPING (`e7`) and for the ADMISSION (the arm A/B flip), and not for
# the guard. S1 inherited its shape from `g7` and its row set from nowhere.
#
# ⇒ THIS FILE IS THE ONE-DIFFERENCE CONTROL: S1 minus the second alternative. Nothing else changes —
# same knot, same holder, same tokens, same layout tier. The candidate is still STARVED (the holder
# still holds `ct` with a residual), the chain is still `ct > prim`, and the guard PGEN emits is
# byte-for-byte the same shape. What changes is that `e1`–`e6` now have exactly ONE route to a parse,
# through `outer_cast`, so each of them tests a guard position:
#
#   e1  k = n'(n);        the LOOP guard must stop the `*` at zero iterations
#   e5  k = t'(n);        the TRAILING guard must refuse the over-long SEED, so `ct_guard`'s other
#                         alternative `kw` wins the tournament and the holder gets its residual back
#
# ⛔ AND IT CANNOT TEST e7. `k = n;` has no holder to reach it here, so it is not in S2's rows — the
# residual-free holder is S1's job. Neither grammar alone measures the design; the PAIR does, which
# is why both ship.
#
# The entry rule is named `scratch` so this file loads into the scratch slot verbatim:
#   cp <this file> grammars/scratch/scratch.ebnf && make -C rust SHELL=/bin/bash focus_scratch
@entry: true
scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi   -> {kind: "held", body: $3}

# ---- identical to S1 from here down.
outer_cast := ct tick lparen lit rparen  -> {type: $1, body: $4}
prim := lit       -> {kind: "lit",  body: $1}
      | cast_expr -> {kind: "cast", body: $1}
cast_expr := ct tick lparen lit rparen   -> {type: $1, body: $4}
ct := kw   -> {kind: "kw",   body: $1}
    | prim -> {kind: "prim", body: $1}

lit := trivia /n/
kw := trivia /t/
kw_k := trivia /k/
eq := trivia "="
semi := trivia ";"
tick := trivia "'"
lparen := trivia "("
rparen := trivia ")"

@branch_policy: priority_first
trivia := (line_comment | block_comment)*
line_comment := /\/\/[^\n]*(\n|$)/
block_comment := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
